# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Qabandi — local development environment.
# Copy to .env and adjust. Never commit .env.
#
# `.env.ci` is the sibling of this file: the configuration every CI job copies
# into place, committed because it holds nothing secret. It is deliberately much
# shorter — it sets only what is the same in every job, and its header explains
# each thing it leaves out.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Database (§5.1) -------------------------------------------------------
# Superuser, used by `pnpm db:provision` to create the database and the two
# application roles on a server where the container entrypoint is not available.
# Never used by the running application; leave unset once provisioned.
ADMIN_DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/postgres

# The application connects as app_user: no BYPASSRLS, no table ownership.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://app_user:app_user@localhost:5432/qabandi
# Migrations only. Owns every object. Never used by web or service at runtime.
MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgres://app_migrator:app_migrator@localhost:5432/qabandi

# Set true behind PgBouncer in transaction-pooling mode.
DATABASE_POOLED=false
DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# TLS to the database. One of `disable`, `require` or `verify-full`.
#
#   verify-full  encrypted, and the certificate checked against DATABASE_CA_CERT.
#                The only setting that survives a hostile network. Prefer it.
#   require      encrypted, certificate not verified. Stops passive capture but
#                not an active man in the middle. Where most managed providers
#                on a private link sit.
#   disable      no TLS, on purpose — the right answer when the database is on
#                a private network the operator controls, and recorded as a
#                decision rather than left to a silence.
#
# Left unset this is fine anywhere the packets stay on the machine, and in
# production against a remote host the process refuses to start. That refusal
# is the point: connecting in clear across somebody's network because nobody
# got round to choosing is not a state this should be able to reach. An
# `sslmode=` already in DATABASE_URL wins outright and turns all of this off.
DATABASE_SSL=disable
# DATABASE_CA_CERT=/etc/ssl/certs/database-ca.pem

# The RLS policy tests need both roles and skip entirely without them (§13).
#
# A database of its own, and not the one DATABASE_URL points at. The suites
# insert fixtures and assert over them, so pointing these at the development
# database means every run writes into the data you were looking at — and on a
# shared server, into the data a colleague was looking at. Against the local
# container the two were the same database for a long time and it was harmless
# because `pnpm db:reset` is free; that stops being true the moment the host is
# anything but localhost.
#
# Create it the same way as the main one, with the name changed:
#   DATABASE_URL=…/qabandi_test MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=…/qabandi_test \
#     pnpm db:provision && pnpm db:migrate && pnpm db:seed
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://app_user:app_user@localhost:5432/qabandi_test
TEST_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgres://app_migrator:app_migrator@localhost:5432/qabandi_test

# --- Auth (§8) -------------------------------------------------------------
# openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=replace-me-with-32-bytes-of-base64
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# How many proxies in front of the app append to X-Forwarded-For. The client is
# taken to be that many entries from the right; anything further left was sent
# by the client and is never believed. 1 = Traefik (the shipped compose file).
# 0 = nothing in front, header ignored. Read by both web and service.
# The web app's proxy writes the address it settles on into
# X-Qabandi-Client-Address on every request (replacing any the caller sent),
# and Better Auth's rate limiter and session rows read only that header — so
# this one setting governs the sign-in throttle, Better Auth and the audit trail.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# --- Brand / whitelabel (NFR-16, FR-CP-01) ---------------------------------
# Every colour, name and mark in the product resolves from @qabandi/brand.
# Nothing below is required: unset, the product is Al-Qabandi. Set any of them
# and the whole interface — screens, PDFs, favicon — re-skins with no rebuild,
# because the tokens are emitted as CSS custom properties at request time.
#
# Onboarding a second operator properly is one file under
# packages/brand/src/brands/, registered in resolve.ts, then BRAND=<key>.
# `pnpm brand:new --key <key> --name-en "<name>" --primary "#rrggbb"` writes
# and registers both, converts the hex, and names every value it had to leave
# as a placeholder. These overrides are for a re-skin between two meetings,
# and for making preview deployments visibly not production.
#
# A BRAND naming a key nobody wrote is now a refusal to start, not a silent
# fallback to the supplier brand. That fallback is how a client deployment
# once wore our identity on every screen and every PDF it issued.
#
# Registered keys: cargosharks (the default, the product's own identity) and
# qabandi (a client). Left unset deliberately — an unconfigured environment
# should wear the supplier's brand, not a client's, because the alternative
# puts somebody else's name on every demo, screenshot and sample PDF. A
# deployment *for* a client sets this explicitly; nothing enforces that at
# boot, so it belongs in the deployment checklist rather than here.
# BRAND=qabandi

# Identity.
# BRAND_NAME_EN="Al-Qabandi & Partners"
# BRAND_NAME_AR="القبندي وشركاه"
# BRAND_TAGLINE_EN="Shipping & Forwarding"
# BRAND_SHORT_NAME=AQP
# BRAND_MONOGRAM=Q

# Palette. Hue is an OKLCH angle 0–360; chroma ~0.10–0.20 for a brand colour.
# Both themes, all three ramps and the chart series are derived from these.
#
# The default (cargosharks) is a blue: primary hue 261 chroma 0.175, accent
# navy hue 256 chroma 0.105. The values below are Al-Qabandi's, taken from
# their live site, and are what `BRAND=qabandi` resolves to:
#   primary  red  #da2726  -> hue 27.4, chroma 0.19
#   accent   navy #1d2143  -> hue 276,  chroma 0.105
# BRAND_PRIMARY_HUE=27.4
# BRAND_PRIMARY_CHROMA=0.19
# BRAND_ACCENT_HUE=276
# BRAND_ACCENT_CHROMA=0.105
# BRAND_RADIUS=10
#
# The five status colours are reserved and do NOT move with the brand —
# positive is green (158), critical is red (27). A brand hue within ~15 degrees
# of one of those reads as that status. (`muted` at 250 is the exception: its
# chroma is 0.02, so its hue is invisible and constrains nothing — which is why
# the default blue at 261 sits eleven degrees away without a problem.)
#
# Al-Qabandi's red sits *on* `critical`, which is why these two exist:
#
#   - the chrome is cut from the accent ramp, so the sidebar is navy rather
#     than four hundred pixels of alarm colour;
#   - the neutral tint follows the accent hue, because tinting every grey with
#     a red brand hue turns the whole interface pink.
#
# Change these together with the primary hue, or not at all.
# BRAND_CHROME_RAMP=accent      # primary | accent | neutral
# BRAND_NEUTRAL_HUE=276
#
# The chart series never uses the brand hue and never a status hue; see
# DEFAULT_CHART_SLOTS in packages/brand/src/palette.ts. Changing a slot means
# re-running the data-viz palette validator against both themes.

# Supply the client's own artwork as an inline square-viewBox SVG; it replaces
# the generated monogram everywhere, including the favicon and PDF letterhead.
# BRAND_LOGO_SVG="<svg viewBox='0 0 48 48'>…</svg>"

# Contact — printed on documents and shown on the customer-facing surfaces.
# BRAND_TRACKING_URL=https://track.qabandi.com
# BRAND_SUPPORT_EMAIL=operations@qabandi.com
# BRAND_SUPPORT_PHONE="+965 2483 0000"

# --- Pilot data (Pilot §3.1 item 14) ---------------------------------------
# The interface runs on the seeded pilot dataset — 20 jobs, a live customs
# hold, a demurrage clock — instead of the database. Defaults to on whenever
# DATABASE_URL is unset, which is what lets the whole thing run on a laptop
# with nothing provisioned, and survive a demo room with no network.
# It refuses to engage in production against a configured database.
#
# Note that `apps/web` reads this file from the *workspace root* — Next only
# looks beside the app, so `next.config.ts` calls the same `loadEnv` the
# scripts use. Without it the app silently ran on fixtures despite a perfectly
# good DATABASE_URL two directories up.
QABANDI_PILOT_FIXTURES=0

# --- Public demo login -----------------------------------------------------
# For ONE deployment: the public demo the marketing site links to. It must
# never be set on a customer's deployment, and it cannot engage without the
# fixtures above — the predicate is `usePilotFixtures() && flag`, so it
# inherits their refusal to run in production against a real database.
#
# Off, the fixtures behave exactly as they always have: session resolution
# short-circuits to the pilot principal and there is no sign-in screen at all.
# That default is not a preference. `pnpm test:e2e` runs its offline suite on
# the assumption that fixtures authenticate nobody and load no storage state,
# so a gate that engaged by default would turn every screen in that suite into
# a redirect to the login form — and would do the same to every developer's
# `QABANDI_PILOT_FIXTURES=1 pnpm dev`.
#
# On, a visitor meets the product's own sign-in screen and picks one of three
# personas, because "what does my customer see, and what does my agent see" is
# the question a forwarder brings to a demo and one login answers a third of
# it. The board, the customer portal and the agent view are three different
# principals with three different scopes:
#
#   operations@cargosharks.example  -> /dashboard  the pilot principal, all keys
#   customer@gulfsteel.example      -> /portal     a contact at the seeded customer
#   agent@everbright.example        -> /agent      a contact at the origin agent
#
# The last two hold no branch, no role and no permission, which is what a real
# customer or agent contact holds — a demo whose portal login could open the
# operations board would disprove the tenancy claim it exists to show.
# QABANDI_DEMO_LOGIN=1
#
# One password for all three, printed on the sign-in screen — a demo whose
# credentials are not on the screen is a demo nobody enters. Unset, it is
# `cargosharks-demo`, so an unconfigured demo still works. Set it where the demo
# should be reachable only by people who were told the password.
# QABANDI_DEMO_PASSWORD=cargosharks-demo

# --- Apps ------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_ENV=development
WEB_URL=http://localhost:3000
SERVICE_URL=http://localhost:3001
SERVICE_PORT=3001

# Shared secret for web -> service calls that are not user-authenticated.
#
# `SERVICE_URL` above must be an *internal* address in production — a container
# name on the private network, plain HTTP, port 3001. Never the service's
# public domain.
#
# The reason is what `/internal` does: this secret authenticates the caller,
# and the caller then names the principal to act as. Anyone holding it can read
# any branch's data. The service has no public domain at all: `/webhooks` (the
# WhatsApp and mail callbacks) and `/api/v1` (the public tracking API) are
# served through the web app, which forwards those two prefixes and nothing
# else. As defence in depth the prefix is also refused when a request arrives
# with proxy forwarding headers.
#
# Deployed from compose.prod.yaml, both containers share one network and this
# is literally the value:                    SERVICE_URL=http://service:3001
# Kept as two separate Coolify applications: enable "Connect to Predefined
#   Network" on both and use the renamed container name, http://service-<uuid>:3001
INTERNAL_API_SECRET=replace-me

# Accept `/internal` calls that arrive with forwarding headers.
#
# Off by default so a misconfigured proxy fails closed. Turn it on only where
# something on the *private* network legitimately adds those headers — a
# service mesh or a sidecar — and never to work around a public route reaching
# the prefix.
INTERNAL_API_TRUST_PROXY=false

# Development only: extra hosts `next dev` will serve its scripts to, comma
# separated — a tunnel such as ngrok used to receive webhooks. Without it a page
# opened through the tunnel renders but its buttons do nothing. Ignored by
# `next start`. Signing in through the tunnel still needs BETTER_AUTH_URL to
# match it; for the browser, prefer http://localhost:3000.
# DEV_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=your-tunnel.ngrok-free.dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The integrations are NOT here. They are rows.
#
# Object storage, the mail relay, the FX feed, the carrier registrations, the
# poll budgets, WhatsApp, the e-invoice provider and the model are configured at
# Settings -> Integrations and stored in `integration_settings` (FR-AD-03),
# with their credentials sealed under MAILBOX_ENCRYPTION_KEY below.
#
# Each still falls back to an environment variable when no row supplies one.
# That fallback is the bootstrap — mail has to work before anybody can sign in
# to configure mail — and the rollback: deactivating a row on the screen
# returns the deployment to exactly what its variables say, with nothing typed
# lost. It is also what keeps this change additive for an installed fleet.
#
# The fallback names are listed in README.md under "Integration settings"
# rather than here, deliberately. A variable in this file reads as *the* place
# to set something, and for these it is the place of last resort — a developer
# who copies this file and fills in an SMTP password has configured the one
# layer that the screen will then silently override.
#
# `pnpm doctor` reports what the environment supplies, and says plainly that
# it cannot see the rows.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Inbound mail -> enquiry (FR-EQ-02, FR-NT-05) ---------------------------
# Which mailboxes this deployment reads is NOT set here. It is `mailboxes`
# rows an administrator edits at Settings -> Mailboxes, because adding the
# Doha office should be a screen rather than a release (FR-AD-03).
#
# What stays here is the one thing that cannot be a row: the key those rows'
# passwords are sealed with. Keeping it in the environment is the whole
# security property — a backup, a restore drill or a replica of the database
# then carries ciphertext and no key. A key stored beside what it protects is
# not encryption.
#
# Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# Leave it blank and the service still boots; the boot line says mailboxes are
# unreadable, and the settings screen refuses to save one.
MAILBOX_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# The poll's schedule is NOT here either — it is a field on the inbound mail
# integration, for the same reason the carrier poll's is: the right frequency
# depends on what the mail host tolerates, which is discovered after the thing
# is running.
#
# The batch size and the attachment ceiling are NOT here any more. They are
# columns on the `mailboxes` row they apply to, edited at Settings ->
# Mailboxes, because they are facts about one mailbox rather than about the
# deployment: a shared Office 365 mailbox on a throttled tenant and a
# self-hosted Dovecot want different answers, and one variable for both meant
# tuning one of them down tuned all five.
#
# UPGRADE NOTE. The column defaults are the values that used to be here — 25
# messages, 25MB — so a deployment that never changed them is unaffected. One
# that *had* tuned either variable must re-enter it per mailbox: these are not
# read as a fallback, because there is nothing deployment-wide left for them
# to be a fallback for.

# --- PDF (§8, NFR-01) ------------------------------------------------------
# Leave blank to use the Chromium that Playwright installs.
CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE_PATH=

# --- Queue (pg-boss, §9) ---------------------------------------------------
PGBOSS_SCHEMA=pgboss

# --- Observability (NFR-12) -------------------------------------------------
# Where traces go. The instrumentation is already in the build — `withActor`
# opens a span per transaction, the service registers the Node SDK, and the web
# app has its own `instrumentation.ts` — but all of it degrades to no-ops
# without an endpoint, because `@opentelemetry/api` does exactly that when no
# SDK is registered. So the code is not the thing standing between this
# deployment and knowing what it is doing; this line is.
#
# Any OTLP/HTTP collector: Grafana Tempo, Jaeger, Honeycomb, a local
# `otel/opentelemetry-collector`. `/v1/traces` is appended for you.
#
# `pnpm doctor` prints whether this is set and what is lost while it is not.
# Left blank, traces are no-ops and errors still reach stderr — which is a
# workable pilot posture and a poor production one, and the reason the gap is
# named rather than left to be discovered.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=qabandi
